  "prediction": "**Summary of the Report on Cobra Dane Radar System**\n\nThe Government Accountability Office (GAO) has released a report on the Cobra Dane radar system, which is used by the Department of Defense (DOD) for various missions, including ballistic missile defense, space surveillance, and intelligence gathering. The report highlights the capabilities of the Cobra Dane system and its contributions to these missions.\n\n**Capabilities and Contributions**\n\nThe Cobra Dane system can track and classify missile threats, provide information to ground-based interceptors, and support space surveillance and intelligence gathering missions. The system's location at Shemya Island, Alaska, provides advantages in tracking and classifying missile threats, particularly from North Korea. The system's capabilities are complemented by other radar systems, such as the Long-Range Discrimination Radar (LRDR), which is located at Clear Air Force Station, Alaska.\n\n**Funding and Sustainment**\n\nThe Air Force and the Missile Defense Agency (MDA) share funding for the operation and sustainment of the Cobra Dane system. The Air Force and MDA plan to spend $278.6 million in fiscal year 2019 on the system's sustainment and modernization. The Air Force has identified three modernization projects for the system, including redesigning parts for the transmitter group, which will cost an estimated $140 million.\n\n**Challenges and Future Plans**\n\nThe report highlights challenges related to the system's operational availability, which has declined from 91% to 88% over the past two years. The Air Force and MDA are working to address these challenges and ensure the system's continued availability. The report also notes that the Pacific Radar and Space Fence may reduce the department's reliance on the Cobra Dane system in the future.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe Cobra Dane radar system plays a critical role in supporting DOD's ballistic missile defense, space surveillance, and intelligence gathering missions. The system's capabilities and contributions are essential to the department's national security objectives. The report highlights the importance of continued funding and sustainment of the system to ensure its continued availability and effectiveness.",
